161
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:43,080
So a DEX, you'd say, has three primary stakeholder groups.

162
00:09:43,080 --> 00:09:45,080
There are traders.

163
00:09:45,080 --> 00:09:50,120
And traders fundamentally just want the best execution on their trades.

164
00:09:50,120 --> 00:09:54,600
They will trade on any DEX as long as that DEX has the tokens that they want to trade

165
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:57,160
and that DEX is giving them the best execution.

166
00:09:57,160 --> 00:09:59,560
So that's one group you are controlling for.

167
00:09:59,560 --> 00:10:02,360
Who provides the liquidity for that?

168
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:04,200
That is liquidity providers.

169
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:08,520
Those are the people coming in who are putting up two sides of any given trade.

170
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:12,760
So your Bitcoin and your USDC.

171
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:16,600
So they create the pool through which all those trades will go through.

172
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:19,160
But they're taking on risk in doing this.

173
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:22,600
The idea of impermanent loss, that the values of those change.

174
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:26,280
That can certainly be something that they're basically taking some risk on.

175
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:28,680
So you need to give them the best rewards.

176
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:31,320
So we're trying to give traders the best execution.

177
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:35,160
We're going to do that by giving liquidity providers the best rewards.

178
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:38,200
And then that third group is basically token holders.

179
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:40,280
DEXs do need tokens.

180
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:41,880
This is very important.

181
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:45,880
We know that because if you go back in the history,

182
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:50,520
