that liquidity.

475
00:28:33,080 --> 00:28:36,760
And if you can do those two things, we know the product is better.

476
00:28:36,760 --> 00:28:44,120
I think it would very quickly flywheel where more volume means more fees, means more TVL.

477
00:28:44,120 --> 00:28:45,560
It really bootstrapping.

478
00:28:45,560 --> 00:28:50,440
And now it's actually deep enough that those FX speculators might actually want to start

479
00:28:50,440 --> 00:28:54,840
coming on-chain and making those markets on-chain as well.

480
00:28:54,840 --> 00:28:59,480
So yeah, I think that's the secret sauce is we've had people building pockets of these

481
00:28:59,480 --> 00:29:04,760
things, but we haven't had both sides of it coming together in one place to execute.

482
00:29:05,400 --> 00:29:09,080
It seems very lucrative for anyone with the assets.

483
00:29:09,080 --> 00:29:14,200
Personally, we're seeing a ton of volume from US to Mexico using stablecoins in the

484
00:29:14,200 --> 00:29:19,080
middle, but you can't do it in fashion that goes through an MXN stablecoin because there's

485
00:29:19,080 --> 00:29:22,280
really no liquidity behind any of these MXN stablecoins.

486
00:29:22,280 --> 00:29:25,960
I had etherfuse on the podcast recently who are trying to do it and I hope they'll be

487
00:29:25,960 --> 00:29:28,360
successful, but still small markets there.

488
00:29:28,360 --> 00:29:33,880
Do you think there will be a traditional finance or mainstream actor that moves into

489
00:29:33,880 --> 00:29:36,280
this opportunity and then we'll get this inflection point?

490
00:29:36,280 --> 00:29:40,440
I think of like a Susquehanna or someone like that who could come in and really move the

491
00:29:40,440 --> 00:29:42,120
scale of the liquidity in these markets.

492
00:29:42,120 --> 00:29:47,880
And then all of a sudden we have robust on-chain MXN, euros, et cetera.

493
00:29:47,880 --> 00:29:50,360
Or what does the zero to one moment look like here?

494
00:29:50,920 --> 00:29:52,920
The answer to the question is absolutely.

495
00:29:52,920 --> 00:29:59,320
And we've talked to, I think, so many institutions who are starting to go down this path.

496
00:29:59,320 --> 00:30:04,680
Regulatory clarity is going to help a lot around the stable bill, but it seems like

497
00:30:04,680 --> 00:30:08,360
the major, major players understand the value prop now.

498
00:30:08,360 --> 00:30:11,320
They're moving their very slow apparatuses.

499
00:30:11,320 --> 00:30:12,680
They're doing their exploration.

500
00:30:12,680 --> 00:30:17,640
So I think there will be that big moment where somebody very, very large comes in.

501
00:30:17,640 --> 00:30:24,120
But in the meantime, I think the growth is still going to be incredible just in onboarding

502
00:30:24,680 --> 00:30:27,640
almost all the smaller fish in relative terms.

503
00:30:27,640 --> 00:30:33,240
And that's going to basically create the proof points to the major players that this is real.

504
00:30:33,240 --> 00:30:38,360
This is useful because there are now businesses putting more and more flows through these.

505
00:30:38,360 --> 00:30:42,280
And again, non-toxic flows, this is real organic usage.

506
00:30:42,280 --> 00:30:47,080
And if they continue to see that drip, drip, drip, someone's going to come in and win

507
00:30:47,080 --> 00:30:48,040
because it's a market.

508
00:30:48,040 --> 00:30:49,080
It's a market to be won.

509
00:30:49,720 --> 00:30:55,160
We continue to see more of these consumer facing apps with crypto in the background.

510
00:30:55,160 --> 00:30:56,280
We have the DeFi mullet.

511
00:30:56,280 --> 00:30:58,520
It's almost like the stablecoin mullet now.

512
00:30:58,520 --> 00:31:04,200
Do you hope that eventually Aerodrome becomes a venue that people interact with less directly

513
00:31:04,200 --> 00:31:10,280
and you become the liquidity venue underpinning FX and underpinning major asset trading?

514
00:31:10,280 --> 00:31:15,640
Do you hope to retain a sizable front end audience or where do you guys hope to sit on the spectrum?

515
00:31:16,200 --> 00:31:19,480
I think about this as like an 80-20 thing.

516
00:31:19,480 --> 00:31:23,160
There's always going to be a need for some degree of a front end,

517
00:31:23,160 --> 00:31:27,960
especially in the way in which we democratize access to all of these things.

518
00:31:27,960 --> 00:31:32,680
We set liquidity providers, blockers of the tokens, voting to direct emissions,

519
00:31:32,680 --> 00:31:34,840
and certainly two-degree traders.

520
00:31:34,840 --> 00:31:37,960
But a DEX is just fundamentally infrastructure.

521
00:31:37,960 --> 00:31:42,200
It's essential infrastructure for a non-chain economy.

522
00:31:42,920 --> 00:31:48,040
Most of the people who will interact with our infrastructure

523
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:51,400
will have no idea that they are interacting with their infrastructure.

524
00:31:51,400 --> 00:31:55,480
They will just be using products like a payments platform,

525
00:31:55,480 --> 00:31:59,560
like a social network with an on-chain economy behind it.

526
00:31:59,560 --> 00:32:04,120
They will just be getting exposure to major digital assets

527
00:32:04,120 --> 00:32:07,080
and they will have no idea how they did it.

528
00:32:07,080 --> 00:32:09,000
Again, going back to the trilemma,

529
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:12,440
they're just going to care about the best execution, the best access to that.

530
00:32:14,360 --> 00:32:17,400
This has been a big difference between us and Uniswap

531
00:32:17,400 --> 00:32:20,920
because I think Uniswap at some point decided that,

532
00:32:20,920 --> 00:32:22,840
hey, we want to be the front end.

533
00:32:22,840 --> 00:32:26,760
We want to be the entry point and we're going to put a lot of time and focus

534
00:32:26,760 --> 00:32:30,600
into being the front end and the entry point to this ecosystem.

535
00:32:31,880 --> 00:32:37,640
I think the issue with that is just that think about who else wants to be the front end.

536
00:32:37,640 --> 00:32:39,480
Coinbase wants to be the front end.

537
00:32:40,200 --> 00:32:42,520
Metamask, lots of people want to be the front end.

538
00:32:43,160 --> 00:32:45,800
Robinhood probably wants to be the front end.

539
00:32:45,800 --> 00:32:50,680
And these are consumer application building companies.

540
00:32:50,680 --> 00:32:54,520
They've got billions of dollars, not in their own token.

541
00:32:54,520 --> 00:32:59,000
They've got billions of real dollars to invest in winning this fight.

542
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:05,000
So why would a crypto company say, I'm going to go kick Coinbase's ass here.

543
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:12,360
I'm going to be Robinhood versus you focus on ensuring that you will be the deepest,

544
00:33:12,360 --> 00:33:14,360
most liquid source of these things.

545
00:33:14,360 --> 00:33:19,240
And no matter who builds the front end, they have to use your liquidity.

546
00:33:19,240 --> 00:33:20,920
So I think that is our vision.

547
00:33:20,920 --> 00:33:25,560
And the other thing about the front end thing is just look at the explosion of AI agents.

548
00:33:25,560 --> 00:33:29,000
Coinbase, Robinhood and seeing every major stock exchange

549
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:31,240
is going to start getting into digital assets.

550
00:33:31,240 --> 00:33:34,200
They're all going to be competing to be the front end of this ecosystem.

551
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:38,280
But you also have crazy new emerging things like natural language,

552
00:33:38,280 --> 00:33:41,640
AI interfaces that might disrupt all of these.

553
00:33:41,640 --> 00:33:43,560
So why go compete over there?

554
00:33:43,560 --> 00:33:48,440
Why not just let them fight it out and ensure that whenever they are doing a swap,

555
00:33:48,440 --> 00:33:51,080
if they want to deliver to their customers the best execution,

556
00:33:51,080 --> 00:33:52,600
they're going to have to use our rails.

557
00:33:52,600 --> 00:33:55,560
So yeah, that is 100% our focus.

558
00:33:55,560 --> 00:34:01,160
We know we'll win if we always have the deepest liquidity in the active tick.

559
00:34:01,160 --> 00:34:07,080
So we should invest all of our R&D, our effort, our economics aligned around that.

560
00:34:07,080 --> 00:34:09,960
Let's not go waste a bunch of time and money

561
00:34:09,960 --> 00:34:13,960
trying to be a better front end than some amazing front ends.

562
00:34:14,680 --> 00:34:21,320
From that perspective, do you look at anyone who can quickly catalyze large-scale liquidity

563
00:34:21,320 --> 00:34:22,840
as potential competition?

564
00:34:22,840 --> 00:34:26,680
Is that what keeps you up at night or do you look at players of that regard

565
00:34:26,680 --> 00:34:30,360
who might be, again, large asset managers, large bank type institutions?

566
00:34:30,360 --> 00:34:31,960
Are they just in a different neck of the woods?

567
00:34:32,520 --> 00:34:36,280
If somebody were to genuinely want to compete with us

568
00:34:36,280 --> 00:34:38,360
for this liquidity advantage,

569
00:34:38,360 --> 00:34:42,680
the interesting thing about the DEX trilemma is just that it's fixed.

570
00:34:43,320 --> 00:34:47,480
And because we reward our LPs with our token

571
00:34:47,480 --> 00:34:49,880
and because we generate all this revenue

572
00:34:49,880 --> 00:34:53,480
and we send all of it out to the token as incentives,

573
00:34:53,480 --> 00:34:58,120
you can't offer them more than 100% of the productive value of the DEX.

574
00:34:58,120 --> 00:34:59,720
There's no greater value problem.

575
00:34:59,720 --> 00:35:03,880
You can offer liquidity providers then 100% of the productive value

576
00:35:03,880 --> 00:35:06,200
and not just today, but in the future.

577
00:35:06,200 --> 00:35:08,840
So we do certainly think about it a lot

578
00:35:08,840 --> 00:35:11,480
and we do think about other ways

579
00:35:11,480 --> 00:35:17,000
in which we can send even more value back to that token as incentives

580
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:21,640
so that the rewards for, again, that active tick liquidity are even higher.

581
00:35:21,640 --> 00:35:26,440
But fundamentally, because we went all the way out in this fixed conditions,

582
00:35:26,440 --> 00:35:30,600
there is nothing anybody could do that would be giving people

583
00:35:30,600 --> 00:35:33,080
more than 100% of the value.

584
00:35:33,080 --> 00:35:36,200
So you'd have to do something basically like us

585
00:35:36,200 --> 00:35:38,520
and then somebody would have to look at it and say,

586
00:35:38,520 --> 00:35:43,480
well, why is this other person delivering 100% of the productive value back

587
00:35:43,480 --> 00:35:47,480
going to beat the network effects of the other team that's doing this

588
00:35:47,480 --> 00:35:51,240
already has the liquidity, has people building on it and all this stuff?

589
00:35:51,240 --> 00:35:54,440
So that's what gives us comfort is we took a big risk, I think,

590
00:35:54,440 --> 00:35:55,560
betting that way.

591
00:35:55,640 --> 00:35:57,080
We didn't raise any money.

592
00:35:57,080 --> 00:35:59,080
We didn't sell any tokens.

593
00:35:59,080 --> 00:36:03,240
We didn't even give team liquid vesting allocations of our tokens.

594
00:36:03,240 --> 00:36:06,280
We just bet on the token is useful

595
00:36:06,280 --> 00:36:09,160
and we will bet our lives on the token being useful.

596
00:36:09,160 --> 00:36:10,840
And that bet's paid off.

597
00:36:10,840 --> 00:36:13,720
But there's really nothing in the DEX trilemma that you can do

598
00:36:13,720 --> 00:36:19,560
that is more than giving 100% of the current and future capitalized value.

599
00:36:19,560 --> 00:36:22,280
You're giving anyone who contributes regardless of who they are

600
00:36:22,280 --> 00:36:24,840
the best value proposition inherently.

601
00:36:24,840 --> 00:36:27,480
And that's what we reward our liquidity providers with

602
00:36:27,480 --> 00:36:29,480
is the token that represents that.

603
00:36:29,480 --> 00:36:31,240
To close out, I wanted to ask you,

604
00:36:31,240 --> 00:36:35,160
what keeps you up at night operating in the spaces you do in the industry?

605
00:36:35,720 --> 00:36:40,840
I think for a long time, especially for me coming into the industry when I did

606
00:36:40,840 --> 00:36:43,960
and Velodrome launching in 2022,

607
00:36:43,960 --> 00:36:49,080
the biggest thing that kept us up at night was just the legal and regulatory overhead.

608
00:36:49,080 --> 00:36:50,040
Don't get me wrong.

609
00:36:50,040 --> 00:36:54,440
I think that there is just a ton of nasty shit in this industry

610
00:36:54,440 --> 00:37:00,120
and I think we need rules and I think we need people enforcing those rules.

611
00:37:00,120 --> 00:37:05,000
And I am hopeful that we'll see real sensible legislation

612
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:09,240
that doesn't treat this industry as the same as a million other industries

613
00:37:09,240 --> 00:37:12,520
that have come before that treats it as a new emergent technology.

614
00:37:12,520 --> 00:37:17,240
But the scariest part of building was that we did everything right.

615
00:37:17,240 --> 00:37:23,320
Or at least as insofar as we could determine what was right, what was legal.

616
00:37:23,320 --> 00:37:26,440
You could ask 100 lawyers the same question and they say,

617
00:37:26,440 --> 00:37:27,960
well, we think you should do this,

618
00:37:27,960 --> 00:37:31,880
but we don't know if an overzealous regulator is going to show up tomorrow and say,

619
00:37:31,880 --> 00:37:34,040
based on the 1910 whatever act,

620
00:37:34,040 --> 00:37:37,560
we actually think this thing is not legal and we're going to ruin your life.

621
00:37:37,560 --> 00:37:44,280
So the biggest thing that's kept me up at night has definitely diminished a lot post-election.

622
00:37:44,280 --> 00:37:47,320
And that I think is a very good thing.

623
00:37:47,320 --> 00:37:52,520
I think the other things that keep me up right now, it's macro just because,

624
00:37:52,520 --> 00:37:55,320
hey guys, we can believe in the vision of the space.

625
00:37:55,320 --> 00:37:56,840
We can believe that we're inevitable.

626
00:37:56,840 --> 00:38:00,200
But unfortunately, we're still furthest out on the risk curve.

627
00:38:00,200 --> 00:38:05,080
And if every major asset class is going to get the ship kicked out of it,

628
00:38:05,080 --> 00:38:07,480
we're going to feel that even more.

629
00:38:07,480 --> 00:38:12,120
But other than those two things, I think just the vision of,

630
00:38:12,120 --> 00:38:18,120
hey, what this technology allows us to do is to build institutional level products

631
00:38:18,120 --> 00:38:21,080
and services free of intermediaries.

632
00:38:21,080 --> 00:38:25,080
It allows us to do that in a way that creates all this surplus value

633
00:38:25,080 --> 00:38:27,240
that can be returned to the commons.

634
00:38:27,240 --> 00:38:31,800
In the end, the history of technology is that the best product wins.

635
00:38:31,800 --> 00:38:38,600
I have no fear that this industry, the technology, the things that we were building will win.

636
00:38:38,600 --> 00:38:44,200
It's just all of the roller coaster of that journey to that mass adoption place.

637
00:38:44,200 --> 00:38:45,800
That's probably a bit painful.

638
00:38:46,440 --> 00:38:47,640
Lots to be excited about.

639
00:38:47,640 --> 00:38:51,320
But to your point, we are still tethered to the platform risk of humanity.

640
00:38:51,880 --> 00:38:53,080
Yes, unfortunately.

641
00:38:53,880 --> 00:38:55,480
Well, Alex, thank you for joining.

642
00:38:55,480 --> 00:38:57,320
Really enjoyed our conversation.

643
00:38:57,320 --> 00:38:59,240
Hope to have you back sometime soon.

644
00:38:59,240 --> 00:38:59,960
Likewise.

645
00:38:59,960 --> 00:39:02,440
Thank you so much for having me.

646
